# Test: the compiled-in owner's child of service stays beside an external augment
#
# Question: when an external module augments the compiled-in container
# `service` with `chrony`, and the config holds both `service dns-resolver`
# (the compiled-in owner's child) and `service chrony`, does the daemon start,
# hand the external plugin its own subtree alone, hand the owner its child,
# and still hold both in the config it runs?
# Why not a unit test: TestJoinAcceptsAugmentOfSharedContainer proves the
# schema holds both children. This proves the daemon parses one `service`
# block that two owners share and cuts the external plugin's root out of it
# without the owner's child.
#
# test/plugin/external-yang-augment-shared-root.ci holds `service chrony`
# alone, so it proves the external half of AC-6 and nothing about the owner.
#
# The owner's half is read from the owner: `show dns resolver` answers the
# config the dns-resolver plugin is running (resolver.show,
# internal/plugins/dnsresolver/show.go), and that plugin answers
# `"enabled": false` with no listener until a config reaches it. The listener
# name, the allow-from count and `"enabled": true` are in the answer only when
# the plugin received `service dns-resolver` out of the shared block.
#
# The resolver serves UDP and TCP on $PORT2. The daemon's SSH server listens
# on TCP $PORT.
#
# VALIDATES: AC-6 of spec-plugin-external-yang-at-startup, "the internal owner
#   of `service` keeps working": its child is accepted and kept beside the
#   augment, and delivery is by declared root.
# PREVENTS: an augment of a shared container that replaces the owner's
#   children, or a delivery that hands the whole `service` block to the
#   external plugin.

option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT
tmpfs=running.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT;
		}
	}
}
bus {
	endpoint acme-chrony {
		external {
			run "le test fixture plugin/external-yang augment";
		}
		encoding json;
	}
}

service {
	dns-resolver {
		enabled true
		listener lan {
			ip 127.0.0.1
			port $PORT2
		}
		allow-from 127.0.0.1/32
	}
	chrony {
		server time.example.net
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze start running.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.
# The external plugin gets its declared root and nothing of the owner's child.
expect=stderr:contains=FIXTURE: external-yang configure root=service/chrony leaves=[service/chrony/server=time.example.net]
reject=stderr:contains=service/dns-resolver
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL

# The wait is on the daemon's own answer, and it retries a refused connection,
# so it also holds the SSH listener.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=le test fixture daemon/await-answer chrony "show config running | json":exit=0

cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "show config running | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains="dns-resolver"
expect=stdout:contains="chrony"
expect=stdout:contains="server": "time.example.net"

# The owner received its child of the shared container and runs it.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show dns resolver | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:contains="running": true
expect=stdout:contains="enabled": true
expect=stdout:contains="name": "lan"
expect=stdout:contains="allow-from": 1
